  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are root nodules?",
      "a": "Root nodules are small swellings on the roots of plants like peas, beans and clover. They house millions of nitrogen-fixing bacteria, and healthy ones are often pink inside.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the lumps on bean roots?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why can't plants use nitrogen from the air?",
      "a": "Nitrogen gas molecules are held together by a very strong triple bond. Only some bacteria have the enzyme, nitrogenase, that can break it and turn nitrogen into ammonia.",
      "asks": [
        "What is nitrogen fixing?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do farmers plant beans and clover?",
      "a": "Beans, peas and clover add nitrogen to the soil through their root bacteria. Growing them in rotation with crops like wheat reduces the need for chemical fertiliser.",
      "asks": [
        "What is crop rotation?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are root nodules pink?",
      "a": "They contain leghaemoglobin, a red protein similar to the haemoglobin in our blood. It controls oxygen, because too much oxygen stops the nitrogen-fixing enzyme working.",
      "asks": [
        "What is leghaemoglobin?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do plants need nitrogen?",
      "a": "Yes. Nitrogen is needed to make proteins, DNA and chlorophyll. Plants short of nitrogen grow slowly and their leaves turn yellow.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do plants need nitrogen?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Air is about 78 percent nitrogen gas, which most living things cannot use directly.",
    "Rhizobium bacteria in the root nodules of legumes such as peas, beans and clover fix nitrogen.",
    "The nitrogen-fixing enzyme nitrogenase is stopped by oxygen, so nodules use leghaemoglobin to control oxygen levels."
  ],
